    Section(
      title: 'DOMAIN B: PROBLEM SOLVING',
      content:
          '**Dual Diagnosis Framework:** Chronic pain post-lumbar fusion + opioid use disorder in sustained remission on medication for opioid use disorder (MOUD).\n\n**OUD Diagnosis (DSM-5 Criteria Met Historically):**\n- Tolerance, withdrawal, use in larger amounts than intended, unsuccessful efforts to cut down, craving, continued use despite physical and psychological harm, social/occupational impairment. Currently in sustained remission on agonist therapy.\n\n**Buprenorphine Pharmacology (Critical Knowledge):**\n- **Mechanism:** Partial mu-opioid agonist and kappa-opioid antagonist. Has a ceiling effect on respiratory depression (safer than full agonists) but DOES provide analgesia.\n- **Analgesic properties:** At 16 mg/day, buprenorphine provides analgesic effect equivalent to approximately 30-40 mg oral morphine equivalents. However, the analgesic duration (6-8 hours) is shorter than the OUD treatment duration (24 hours), which is why some patients on stable MOUD still report pain.\n- **Drug interactions in rehab setting:** Do NOT administer full mu-opioid agonists (morphine, oxycodone, hydromorphone) — they will be blocked by buprenorphine at the receptor and could precipitate withdrawal or be ineffective. Tramadol is contraindicated.\n- **Key principle:** Buprenorphine should NEVER be discontinued for pain management during rehab. Stopping MOUD dramatically increases relapse risk and is the single most dangerous action.\n\n**Pain Assessment Considerations:**\n- Chronic pain in OUD patients requires multidimensional assessment — pain severity, functional impact, psychological contributors (anxiety, catastrophizing), and distinction from opioid craving\n- Use validated tools: Brief Pain Inventory, PCS (Pain Catastrophizing Scale), SOAPP-R for ongoing risk assessment\n- His pain at 5-7/10 is expected post-fusion with deconditioning and is manageable with multimodal approach',
