      content:
          '**Q: The patient reports his pain has increased significantly since admission (now 8/10). How do you differentiate undertreated pain from opioid craving or pseudo-addiction?**\n\n**A:** This is one of the most challenging clinical scenarios in rehabilitation medicine:\n\n**Systematic Assessment:**\n1. **Objective pain generators:** Examine for new surgical complications (wound dehiscence, hardware failure, infection recurrence). Order imaging if clinical concern. Increased activity in rehab is an expected cause of increased pain.\n2. **Buprenorphine adequacy:** Consider splitting the dose — 8 mg BID instead of 16 mg daily. The analgesic effect lasts 6-8 hours while the anti-craving effect lasts 24 hours. Splitting improves around-the-clock pain coverage without increasing total dose.\n3. **Craving vs pain:** Ask directly — "Are you experiencing any urges to use?" Patients on stable MOUD appreciate direct, non-judgmental inquiry. Craving presents as restlessness, anxiety, irritability, and is relieved by addressing the craving (counseling, distraction), not by analgesics. Pain is typically localized, worsened by specific movements, and improved by rest/positioning.\n4. **Pseudo-addiction pattern:** If the patient is requesting medications early, appearing anxious about the next dose, or "clock-watching" — this may represent inadequate pain control rather than addiction behavior. The appropriate response is to optimize multimodal analgesia, NOT to restrict medications.\n5. **UDS monitoring:** Continue routine urine drug screens per facility protocol — but present this as standard care for all patients on controlled substances, not as surveillance specifically because of his OUD history.\n6. **Collaborative approach:** Contact his addiction medicine provider to discuss dosing adjustments — they should remain involved throughout the rehab stay.',
