          '**Multimodal Non-Opioid Pain Management:**\n- **Maintain buprenorphine** at current dose (16 mg daily) or split to 8 mg BID for improved analgesic coverage\n- **Acetaminophen** 1000 mg TID (scheduled, not PRN — ensures consistent baseline analgesia)\n- **Gabapentin** 600 mg TID (already on this — provides neuropathic and anxiolytic benefit)\n- **NSAIDs:** Consider short-course meloxicam 15 mg daily if no surgical contraindication (check with surgeon regarding fusion healing)\n- **Topical agents:** Lidocaine 5% patches to low back, diclofenac gel to paraspinals\n- **Cyclobenzaprine:** Continue 10 mg qHS for nocturnal spasm and sleep benefit\n- **Non-pharmacological (critical):**\n  - Graded therapeutic exercise (the most effective intervention for post-fusion pain)\n  - Heat/ice modalities\n  - TENS unit\n  - Cognitive behavioral therapy for pain (CBT-P) — evidence-based for chronic pain with OUD\n  - Mindfulness-based stress reduction\n  - Biofeedback for muscle tension and pain coping\n\n**Maintaining MOUD During Rehab:**\n- Buprenorphine must be administered by licensed pharmacy at the facility\n- Continue prescribed dose without interruption — document in admission orders as priority medication\n- Do NOT hold buprenorphine for procedures, fasting, or any other reason without addiction medicine consultation\n- Coordinate with outpatient prescriber for continuity\n\n**Addiction Counseling Integration:**\n- Continue weekly group counseling (telehealth if in-person meetings not available during rehab)\n- Encourage NA meeting attendance via telephone or virtual while inpatient\n- Sponsor contact should be facilitated — allow phone access for recovery support calls\n- Psychology consult for integrated pain/addiction counseling during rehab stay\n\n**Relapse Prevention During Rehab:**\n- Identify triggers: pain, boredom, isolation from recovery community, exposure to other patients\' pain medications\n- Ensure roommate compatibility — avoid rooming with patients on high-dose opioid regimens if possible\n- Maintain structured daily schedule to minimize unoccupied time\n- Naloxone rescue kit should be part of discharge planning',
