          '**Regulatory Framework for Buprenorphine in Rehab (Updated 2023):**\n- **X-waiver elimination:** As of January 2023 (Consolidated Appropriations Act), the X-waiver requirement was eliminated. ANY physician with a standard DEA license can prescribe buprenorphine for OUD. The previous barrier of needing a special DATA 2000 waiver no longer exists.\n- **Practical implication:** The rehab attending can prescribe and adjust buprenorphine directly, though coordination with the outpatient addiction medicine provider is best practice.\n- **Facility requirements:** The rehab facility pharmacy must stock buprenorphine/naloxone. Some facilities may have institutional barriers — advocate for formulary inclusion if not available.\n\n**Harm Reduction Resources:**\n- Naloxone (Narcan) nasal spray prescription for discharge — available OTC in most states but ensure insurance coverage\n- Fentanyl test strips — provide information if returning to environment with potential exposure\n- Clean syringe access information (if applicable) — harm reduction does not equal endorsement of use\n\n**SAMHSA Resources:**\n- SAMHSA National Helpline: 1-800-662-4357\n- SAMHSA treatment locator (findtreatment.gov) for outpatient MOUD continuity\n- Ensure discharge plan includes confirmed appointment with addiction medicine provider within 7 days of discharge\n\n**Insurance and Coverage:**\n- Mental Health Parity and Addiction Equity Act requires insurers to cover MOUD at parity with medical/surgical benefits\n- Prior authorization for buprenorphine should not delay treatment initiation\n- If insurance barriers exist, manufacturer patient assistance programs and state-funded MOUD programs available\n\n**Rehab Facility Policy Considerations:**\n- Facility should have a MOUD policy — if none exists, advocate for creation\n- Staff education on MOUD is often inadequate and drives stigma\n- Ensure pharmacy can verify buprenorphine prescriptions and coordinate with external providers\n- Develop protocol for patients on methadone (requires coordination with opioid treatment program for daily dosing)',
