    Section(
      title: '+ Systems question',
      content:
          '**Q: The facility medical director says they have a policy against administering buprenorphine in the rehab unit because "it is an opioid and sends the wrong message to other patients." How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** This requires advocacy grounded in law, ethics, and evidence:\n\n1. **Legal mandate:** Under the ADA and Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act, patients on MOUD for OUD are considered individuals with a disability receiving treatment. Refusing to administer prescribed MOUD is **disability discrimination** and exposes the facility to legal liability. Multiple DOJ settlements have established this precedent.\n\n2. **Medical standard of care:** MOUD (buprenorphine, methadone, naltrexone) is the gold standard for OUD treatment, endorsed by WHO, ASAM, APA, and SAMHSA. Discontinuing MOUD increases relapse risk 3-5 fold and overdose death risk significantly. Not administering prescribed MOUD is a patient safety issue.\n\n3. **Evidence rebuttal:** "Sending the wrong message" reflects stigma, not science. Buprenorphine is FDA-approved medication treating a chronic brain disease. It is analogous to refusing insulin for diabetes because it "sends the wrong message about diet." Other patients\' perceptions are managed through confidentiality, not by withholding treatment.\n\n4. **Practical solution:** Propose a facility MOUD policy that includes staff education, standardized administration protocols, patient confidentiality protections, and coordination with addiction medicine providers. Offer to lead the educational initiative.\n\n5. **Escalation pathway:** If the medical director is intractable, escalate to facility administration, compliance/legal department, and if necessary, state medical board or advocacy organizations (Legal Action Center specializes in MOUD discrimination cases).',
