      content:
          '**Q: The patient\'s wife asks to speak with you privately. She says, "I support his recovery, but I\'m terrified of having any opioid medications in our house when he comes home. Can\'t he just stop the Suboxone before discharge?" How do you respond?**\n\n**A:** Her fear is valid and reflects the trauma of living with someone with OUD. This requires empathetic education:\n\n"Your concern shows how much you care about his recovery, and I want to acknowledge how difficult the past few years must have been for you. Living with a loved one\'s addiction is traumatic, and your fears are completely understandable.\n\nHowever, I need to share some important medical information about buprenorphine that I think will ease your concern:\n\n1. **Buprenorphine is fundamentally different from the opioids that were a problem.** It has a ceiling effect — it cannot produce the high that oxycodone or fentanyl does. At his prescribed dose, it blocks euphoria from other opioids. It is not a drug of abuse for him; it is the medication preventing relapse.\n\n2. **Stopping buprenorphine is the most dangerous thing we could do.** Studies show that people who stop MOUD have a 3 to 5 times higher risk of relapse and a dramatically higher risk of fatal overdose. His brain chemistry has been altered by years of opioid exposure, and buprenorphine normalizes it. Removing it would be like stopping blood pressure medication because someone is afraid of pills.\n\n3. **How long will he need it?** The current evidence supports long-term, potentially indefinite MOUD maintenance. There is no \'right\' time to stop. His addiction medicine doctor will reassess periodically, but right now the priority is keeping him stable.\n\n4. **Safety at home:** The buprenorphine films should be stored securely — locked box, away from children. But the risk of diversion is low because he needs it for his own treatment.\n\n5. **For you:** I strongly recommend family counseling or Al-Anon/Nar-Anon meetings if you are not already attending. Your recovery and his recovery are connected but separate processes. You deserve support too.\n\nWould it be helpful to have a joint meeting with his addiction medicine doctor before discharge so all your questions can be addressed together?"',
